    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Shansabani chiefs from the mountains of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ghor">Ghor</a> rose from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ghaznavid-empire">Ghaznavid</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#vassal">vassals</a> to rulers of an empire from Khurasan to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#bengal">Bengal</a>.</li><li>Mu'izz al-Din won the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#second-battle-of-tarain">Second Battle of Tarain</a> in 1192, and his slave generals went on to found the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#delhi-sultanate">Delhi Sultanate</a>.</li><li>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#minaret-of-jam">Minaret of Jam</a>, about 65 metres high, survives in a remote Afghan valley as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>In northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#india">India</a> the Ghurid sultan Mu'izz al-Din Muhammad issued gold coins bearing the image of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#hinduism">Hindu</a> goddess <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#lakshmi">Lakshmi</a>, following local coin designs.</p></aside>
    </section>

