    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#mahmud-of-ghazni"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mahmud-of-ghazni.jpg" alt="Mahmud of Ghazni" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#mahmud-of-ghazni">Mahmud of Ghazni</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mahmud_of_Ghazni,_Ghaznavid_ruler,_conquering_Qasdar_(modern_Khuzdar)_in_India,_miniature_from_the_Jami%CA%BF_al-Tawarikh_of_Rashid_al-Din_Il-Khanid_Tabriz_Ms_Or_20_f.108v.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">1314-15, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ghor">Ghor</a>, a remote and mountainous region of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#afghanistan">Afghanistan</a> between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#herat">Herat</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ghazni">Ghazni</a>, was among the last parts of the eastern Iranian world to accept <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#islam">Islam</a>, and its people lived in fortified villages under local chiefs. Little is known of its early history. Later Ghurid tradition, recorded by the historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#minhaj-i-siraj-juzjani">Minhaj al-Siraj Juzjani</a> in the thirteenth century, traced the ruling Shansabani family back to legendary Iranian kings and to an ancestor said to have converted at the hands of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#caliphate">caliph</a> Ali; the starting date of 879 used here is conventional, since no reliable record of the dynasty survives from that time. The family becomes visible in 1011, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mahmud-of-ghazni">Mahmud of Ghazni</a> invaded Ghor and captured its chief, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#muhammad-ibn-suri">Muhammad ibn Suri</a>. For more than a century afterwards the Shansabanids were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#vassal">vassals</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ghaznavid-empire">Ghaznavids</a> and, from the early twelfth century, of the Seljuk sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ahmad-sanjar">Sanjar</a>. The break came in the 1140s, when the Ghaznavid sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#bahram-shah-of-ghazna">Bahram Shah</a> had the Ghurid prince Qutb al-Din Muhammad poisoned and later executed his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sayf-al-din-suri">Sayf al-Din Suri</a>, who had briefly seized Ghazni. In revenge another brother, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ala-al-din-husayn">Ala al-Din Husayn</a>, defeated Bahram Shah and in 1150 or 1151 sacked Ghazni, burning it for seven days, which earned him the name <em>Jahan-suz</em>, 'World Burner'.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#herat"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/herat.jpg" alt="Herat" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#herat">Herat</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Herat_7452a.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Yves Picq http://veton.picq.fr, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ghor">Ghor</a> itself was poor: a land of high valleys, pastures and forests where farming and stock raising supported a population of hardy highlanders, and whose main exports were reportedly slaves, armour and weapons made from local iron. The empire's wealth came from conquest. The Ghurids came to control the cities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#herat">Herat</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ghazni">Ghazni</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#balkh">Balkh</a>, the trade routes between Khurasan and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#india">India</a> and, from the 1190s, the rich farmland of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#punjab">Punjab</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ganges-plain">Ganges plain</a>, whose treasuries and temples yielded enormous booty. Ghurid coinage reflects this mixed empire. In northern India Mu'izz al-Din issued coins in the local style, including gold coins bearing the image of the goddess <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#lakshmi">Lakshmi</a> and billon coins with a bull and a horseman, while in the west the sultans struck Islamic dinars and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#dirham">dirhams</a>. Religion was a field of change. Many highlanders of Ghor had followed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#karramiyya">Karramiyya</a>, a pietist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sunni-islam">Sunni</a> movement, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ghiyath-al-din-muhammad">Ghiyath al-Din Muhammad</a> later adopted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#shafii-school">Shafi'i school</a> while Mu'izz al-Din became a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#hanafi-school">Hanafi</a>, and the theologian Fakhr al-Din al-Razi was active at Ghurid courts. Buildings and works of art show a court culture that drew on Persian traditions and employed craftsmen from across the eastern Islamic lands, with Herat as its leading city.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-kasahrada"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-kasahrada.jpg" alt="Battle of Kasahrada" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-kasahrada">Battle of Kasahrada</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kasahrada.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Sajaypal007, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Ghurid armies combined the infantry of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ghor">Ghor</a>, known for fighting on foot behind large shields, with Turkic slave cavalry (<em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ghulam">ghulams</a></em>), <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#khalaj">Khalaj</a>, Afghan and other tribal contingents and, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#india">India</a>, captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#war-elephant">war elephants</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mounted-archer">Mounted archers</a> and the Turkic military slaves who commanded them formed the striking force. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ghiyath-al-din-muhammad">Ghiyath al-Din Muhammad</a> (r. 1163–1203) the brothers fought on two fronts. In the west they took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#herat">Herat</a> around 1175 and contested Khurasan with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#khwarazmian-empire">Khwarazmshahs</a>, briefly occupying <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#nishapur">Nishapur</a> after the death of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#khwarazmshah">Khwarazmshah</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ala-ad-din-tekish">Tekish</a> in 1200. In the east Mu'izz al-Din took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ghazni">Ghazni</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#oghuz-turks">Ghuzz Turkmen</a> in 1173 and from there advanced into India. He captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#multan">Multan</a> in 1175 but was defeated by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#chaulukya-dynasty">Chaulukyas of Gujarat</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-kasahrada">Kasahrada</a> in 1178, and he ended <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ghaznavid-empire">Ghaznavid</a> rule in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#punjab">Punjab</a> by taking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#lahore">Lahore</a> in 1186. In 1191 a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#rajputs">Rajput</a> coalition under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#prithviraj-chauhan">Prithviraj Chauhan</a> defeated him at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#first-battle-of-tarain">the First Battle of Tarain</a>, but the next year, at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#second-battle-of-tarain">Second Battle of Tarain</a>, his mobile cavalry won a decisive victory. In 1194 he defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#gahadavala-dynasty">Gahadavala</a> king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#jayachandra">Jayachandra</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-chandawar">Battle of Chandawar</a>, and his commanders went on to raid as far as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#bihar">Bihar</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#bengal">Bengal</a>. In 1204, however, a Khwarazmian army supported by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#qara-khitai">Qara Khitai</a> crushed him at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-andkhud">Andkhud</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#ghiyath-al-din-muhammad"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ghiyath-al-din-muhammad.jpg" alt="Ghiyath al-Din Muhammad" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#ghiyath-al-din-muhammad">Ghiyath al-Din Muhammad</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ghiyath_al-Din_Muhammad.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Ninara, CC BY 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Ghurid realm was a family enterprise rather than a centralised state. From 1163 the brothers <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ghiyath-al-din-muhammad">Ghiyath al-Din Muhammad</a> and Mu'izz al-Din Muhammad shared power in a partnership that held for forty years. Ghiyath al-Din, the senior sultan, ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#firozkoh">Firozkoh</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ghor">Ghor</a> and from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#herat">Herat</a> and directed the western wars, while Mu'izz al-Din, governing from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ghazni">Ghazni</a> from 1173, led the conquests in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#india">India</a>, acknowledging his brother on his coins and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#khutba">Friday prayer</a>. Another branch of the Shansabani family held Bamiyan and the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#oxus">Oxus</a> lands as a semi-independent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#appanage">appanage</a>. Below the dynasty, power rested with military commanders. In India Mu'izz al-Din relied on Turkic slave generals whom he had bought and trained, such as Qutb al-Din <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#aybak">Aybak</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#delhi">Delhi</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#lahore">Lahore</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#taj-al-din-yildiz">Taj al-Din Yildiz</a> at Kirman and Ghazni, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#nasir-ad-din-qabacha">Nasir al-Din Qabacha</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#multan">Multan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#uch-sharif">Uch</a>, together with free commanders such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#bakhtiyar-khalji">Muhammad Bakhtiyar Khalji</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#bihar">Bihar</a>. Conquered Indian lands were divided into revenue assignments that supported the troops, and many local <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#hinduism">Hindu</a> rulers were left in place as tributaries. The Ghurids adopted Persian administrative practice, Persian as the language of government and the titles of Islamic kingship, and cultivated recognition from the Abbasid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#caliphate">caliph</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#baghdad">Baghdad</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#minaret-of-jam"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/minaret-of-jam.jpg" alt="Minaret of Jam" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#minaret-of-jam">Minaret of Jam</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jam_afghan_architecture_harirud_brick.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">david adamec, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Although it was a great power for barely half a century, the Ghurid Sultanate changed the history of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#south-asia">South Asia</a>. Mu'izz al-Din's victories at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#second-battle-of-tarain">Tarain</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-chandawar">Chandawar</a> broke the leading <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#rajputs">Rajput</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#gahadavala-dynasty">Gahadavala</a> kingdoms of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ganges-plain">Ganges plain</a>, and his commanders created the military and administrative framework from which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#delhi-sultanate">Delhi Sultanate</a> emerged in 1206. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#aybak">Aybak</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#iltutmish">Iltutmish</a> and other Turkic commanders of Ghurid origin ruled northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#india">India</a> for most of the thirteenth century, and the Muslim dynasties that dominated much of the subcontinent for the next five centuries trace their beginnings to the Ghurid conquest. The conquest also brought destruction: temples were demolished and their materials reused, as in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#quwwat-ul-islam-mosque">Quwwat al-Islam mosque</a> begun at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#delhi">Delhi</a> in the 1190s, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#bakhtiyar-khalji">Bakhtiyar Khalji</a>'s raids in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#bihar">Bihar</a> are traditionally associated with the sack of Buddhist monasteries such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#nalanda">Nalanda</a>. The Ghurids were also major builders. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#minaret-of-jam">Minaret of Jam</a>, about 65 metres high and decorated with brick patterns and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#quran">Qur'anic</a> chapter of Maryam, was raised in the remote valley that probably held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#firozkoh">Firozkoh</a>, most likely in the 1190s, and is now a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>. Ghiyath al-Din rebuilt the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#friday-mosque-of-herat">Friday Mosque of Herat</a>, and Aybak began the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#qutb-minar">Qutb Minar</a> at Delhi, which Iltutmish completed.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#ala-ad-din-muhammad-ii"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ala-ad-din-muhammad-ii.jpg" alt="Ala ad-Din Muhammad II" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#ala-ad-din-muhammad-ii">Ala ad-Din Muhammad II</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Muhammad_II_portrait_in_a_1430_manuscript_of_the_Jami%27_al-tawarikh_by_Rashid-al-Din_Hamadani.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Jami' al-tawarikh, Rashid al-Din, 15th century, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Ghurid empire depended on the partnership of the two brothers and could not survive their deaths. Ghiyath al-Din died in 1203, leaving Mu'izz al-Din as sole sultan, and the disaster at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-andkhud">Andkhud</a> in 1204 against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ala-ad-din-muhammad-ii">Khwarazmshah Muhammad II</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#qara-khitai">Qara Khitai</a> encouraged rebellion. After suppressing a rising of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#khokhars">Khokhars</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#punjab">Punjab</a>, Mu'izz al-Din was assassinated in March 1206 at Damyak in the Punjab, either by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ismailism">Isma'ili</a> agents or by Khokhars; the sources disagree. He left no son, and the empire broke into rival domains. Ghiyath al-Din's son Ghiyath al-Din Mahmud took the throne at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#firozkoh">Firozkoh</a>, the Bamiyan branch contested the inheritance, and the slave commanders made themselves masters of their provinces: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#taj-al-din-yildiz">Yildiz</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ghazni">Ghazni</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#nasir-ad-din-qabacha">Qabacha</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#multan">Multan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#aybak">Aybak</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#lahore">Lahore</a>, where he founded what became the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#delhi-sultanate">Delhi Sultanate</a>. Ghiyath al-Din Mahmud, squeezed between rivals, was murdered in 1212. Meanwhile the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#khwarazmshah">Khwarazmshah</a> Muhammad II expanded into Khurasan and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>, absorbed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ghor">Ghor</a> and took Ghazni by 1215, ending Ghurid rule; Yildiz fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#india">India</a>, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#iltutmish">Iltutmish</a> defeated him in 1216. A few years later Ghor itself was devastated in the Mongol invasion of the early 1220s, and Firozkoh was destroyed so thoroughly that its site was long forgotten.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Ghurid Sultanate rose from the mountains of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#afghanistan">Afghanistan</a> to rule from Khurasan to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#bengal">Bengal</a> in the late twelfth century; its conquest of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#india">India</a> under Mu'izz al-Din Muhammad laid the foundations of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#delhi-sultanate">Delhi Sultanate</a>, and its monuments include the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#minaret-of-jam">Minaret of Jam</a>.</em></p>

